WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.760
Instructor: Alright, so in the last few videos we've seen

00:02.760 --> 00:06.450
that we can manually tell GIT to store characters,

00:06.450 --> 00:09.120
single strings like hello and goodbye

00:09.120 --> 00:12.240
and it will store them in the objects directory.

00:12.240 --> 00:14.283
It creates a file using the hash.

00:14.283 --> 00:17.640
So here I'm just showing, you know the, the hash file.

00:17.640 --> 00:19.170
I'm not showing the subdirectory.

00:19.170 --> 00:21.743
It's nested inside of technically the hash.

00:21.743 --> 00:24.600
The first two characters make the directory name right

00:24.600 --> 00:26.763
and then the other 38 make up the file name.

00:26.763 --> 00:30.873
But anyway, it stores the contents in this case, hello,

00:30.873 --> 00:35.280
in the file and it names it with this key with the hash.

00:35.280 --> 00:37.680
And then I can put something else in there like goodbye

00:37.680 --> 00:38.760
and chicken.

00:38.760 --> 00:41.466
Or I could have different versions of files,

00:41.466 --> 00:42.974
which is more realistic.

00:42.974 --> 00:46.440
I can have a hundred lines in a file versus a thousand lines

00:46.440 --> 00:49.320
in the same file or store 10 different files.

00:49.320 --> 00:52.230
Git is going to create objects for each of them. And then,

00:52.230 --> 00:55.470
at any point, we can manually say, Hey, here's the key

00:55.470 --> 00:58.980
for something, please go give me the corresponding data.

00:58.980 --> 01:01.320
And we saw Git was able to do that.

01:01.320 --> 01:04.860
So at this point, everything that we have stored

01:04.860 --> 01:08.400
all of the stuff in this objects directory,

01:08.400 --> 01:10.320
they are examples of one type

01:10.320 --> 01:13.260
of a Git object called blobs,

01:13.260 --> 01:16.188
binary large objects blob.

01:16.188 --> 01:19.380
So this is one type of an object Git can store.

01:19.380 --> 01:23.220
Remember there are four. Commits are one, blobs are another.

01:23.220 --> 01:24.930
Something called trees, we'll see next.

01:24.930 --> 01:26.730
And then annotated tags.

01:26.730 --> 01:28.320
We don't care about those so much.

01:28.320 --> 01:31.650
But really blobs, commits and trees are the most important.

01:31.650 --> 01:36.330
So a blob is used to store the content of a file.

01:36.330 --> 01:40.140
It is just the content of a file, just the insides.

01:40.140 --> 01:42.158
It doesn't include even the file name

01:42.158 --> 01:44.100
it's just the content.

01:44.100 --> 01:48.270
So what we've done so far is created a couple of blobs.

01:48.270 --> 01:50.550
This is a blob, this is a blob, this is a blob.

01:50.550 --> 01:51.581
This is a blob.

01:51.581 --> 01:55.290
They are just content that we've stored.

01:55.290 --> 01:56.580
Now what's interesting, as I said

01:56.580 --> 01:59.160
is that the file name is not included.

01:59.160 --> 02:01.320
So you can visualize a blob like this.

02:01.320 --> 02:05.340
This is the content in a file, some JavaScript file.

02:05.340 --> 02:08.250
Git of course keeps track of a file name,

02:08.250 --> 02:09.675
and that's where trees come in.

02:09.675 --> 02:14.261
But Git stores the contents of a file separate from the name

02:14.261 --> 02:17.760
and then each blob gets its own hash.

02:17.760 --> 02:19.230
We've already seen that.

02:19.230 --> 02:20.190
These are blobs.

02:20.190 --> 02:21.780
We created a blob.

02:21.780 --> 02:23.280
It got this hash.

02:23.280 --> 02:26.730
This is a blob, it has this hash, 39 E 275.

02:26.730 --> 02:29.940
This is a blob, CE 0136.

02:29.940 --> 02:31.145
Blobs are a building block

02:31.145 --> 02:34.866
of the way that we use gits, where we have different files

02:34.866 --> 02:38.190
and different contents and different versions and commits.

02:38.190 --> 02:40.915
They play a very important role, as you'll see

02:40.915 --> 02:44.370
but on their own, a blob is just a Git object

02:44.370 --> 02:46.770
that stores the contents of a file.

02:46.770 --> 02:48.300
Doesn't even include the file name

02:48.300 --> 02:49.980
but it does get its own hash.

02:49.980 --> 02:52.320
So it's not a commit hash, it's a blob hash.

02:52.320 --> 02:54.179
They might look like a commit hash, but that's just

02:54.179 --> 02:57.540
because they're all 40 character hexa decimals, right?

02:57.540 --> 02:59.160
It's impossible to tell them apart

02:59.160 --> 03:03.000
and they're the output of the same shah one function.

03:03.000 --> 03:05.010
Anyway, that's an intro to blobs.

03:05.010 --> 03:08.013
In the next video we get to talk about trees.
